{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-136156","model_call":25,"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ chronic stress hippocampus memory impairment)"},"record":{"query":{"op":"query","query":"(^ chronic stress hippocampus memory impairment)"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_17_539571482#7_585162105","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q1215","rank":1,"snippet":"deprivation stress Child neglect Cognitive reserve Aging Prenatal and perinatal cocaine exposure Humans Localized cerebral cortex changes Institutional deprivation Cognitive reserve and resilience See also Notes Bibliography External links \" , \"segment\": \"Research on rats learning an “acrobatic” skill shows that such learning activity leads to increased synapse count. Maternal transmission Environmental enrichment during pregnancy has effects upon the fetus, such as accelerating his or her retinal development. Neurogenesis Environmental enrichment can also lead to the formation of neurons (at least in rats) and reverse both the loss of neurons in the <cover>hippocampus and memory impairment from chronic stress. </cover>However, its relevance has been questioned for the behavioral effects of enriched environments. Mechanisms Enriched environments affect the expression of genes that determine neuronal structure in the cerebral cortex and hippocampus. At the molecular level, this occurs through increased concentrations of the neurotrophins NGF, NT-3, and changes in BDNF. This alters the activation of cholinergic neurons, 5-HT, and beta-adrenolin. Another effect is to increase proteins such as synaptophysin and PSD-95 in synapses. Changes in Wnt signaling have also been found to mimic in adult mice the effects of environmental enrichment upon synapses in "},"time":205},"model_calls":25,"document_judgments":18,"relevance_3":7,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
