{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-136156","model_call":30,"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"cortisol levels\" \"hippocampal atrophy\" \"memory deficits\")"},"record":{"query":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"cortisol levels\" \"hippocampal atrophy\" \"memory deficits\")"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_08_326333977#3_599475270","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q1218","rank":1,"snippet":"References Author information Affiliations Rights and permissions About this article Cite this article Share this article Further reading Stress- and trauma-related blockade of episodic-autobiographical memory processing Does the Stress of Laboratory Life and Experimentation on Animals Adversely Affect Research Data? A Critical Review Acute Effects of Tai Chi Training on Cognitive and Cardiovascular Responses in Late Middle-Aged Adults: A Pilot Study The caffeine-binding adenosine A2A receptor induces age-like HPA-axis dysfunction by targeting glucocorticoid receptor function Biological correlates of adult cognition: Midlife in the United States (MIDUS) Associated Content<cover>Cortisol levels during human aging predict hippocampal atrophy and memory deficits</cover>\" , \"segment\": \"The authors report that prolonged elevation of cortisol, the major stress hormone in humans, correlates with hippocampal damage and related cognitive deficits in aged volunteers. A profile of high and rising cortisol levels over five years was linked to a 14% decrease in hippocampal volume compared to age-matched controls whose cortisol levels were lower. Thus, this study takes a major step toward generalizing and broadening the clinical implications of the glucocorticoid hypothesis of brain aging. Glucocorticoids, primarily cortisol and corticosterone, are produced by the adrenal gland in "},"time":47},"model_calls":30,"document_judgments":23,"relevance_3":8,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
