{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-136156","model_call":31,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1218"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q1218"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_08_326333977#2_599472256","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1218","rank":2,"snippet":"References Author information Affiliations Rights and permissions About this article Cite this article Share this article Further reading Stress- and trauma-related blockade of episodic-autobiographical memory processing Does the Stress of Laboratory Life and Experimentation on Animals Adversely Affect Research Data? A Critical Review Acute Effects of Tai Chi Training on Cognitive and Cardiovascular Responses in Late Middle-Aged Adults: A Pilot Study The caffeine-binding adenosine A2A receptor induces age-like HPA-axis dysfunction by targeting glucocorticoid receptor function Biological correlates of adult cognition: Midlife in the United States (MIDUS) Associated Content<cover>Cortisol levels during human aging predict hippocampal atrophy and memory deficits</cover>\" , \"segment\": \"One longstanding hypothesis suggests that glucocorticoid stress hormones increase neuronal vulnerability to age-related damage. This hypothesis attributes cognitive decline with aging to prolonged glucocorticoid exposure in the hippocampus. Consistent with this possibility, both normal aging and Alzheimer's disease strongly affect hippocampal neurons 1, 2, 3, which are essential for learning and memory 4 and rich in glucocorticoid receptors 5. Evidence has been accumulating steadily for 20 years that glucocorticoids promote hippocampal aging in rodents 6, 7, 8, 9, 10, 11, but whether these findings also apply "},"time":11},"model_calls":31,"document_judgments":24,"relevance_3":9,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
