{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-136156","model_call":32,"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"stress before\" \"memory retrieval\" impairs recall)"},"record":{"query":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"stress before\" \"memory retrieval\" impairs recall)"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_48_36044806#8_82268102","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q1219","rank":1,"snippet":"levels returned to baseline and genomic cortisol actions are at work. 18, 19 Moreover, distinct memory stages, i.e., encoding, consolidation or retrieval may be differently affected by these time-dependent physiological changes after a stressful encounter. In the next section, we will portray the time-dependent effects of stress on learning and memory, taking into account both the specific memory stage affected and the temporal proximity between the stressful event and the memory formation or retrieval process ( Figure 2 ). Figure 2 The effects of stress on memory depend on the specific memory process investigated and the temporal proximity between the stressful event and this memory process. While stress (indicated as red flash) long before encoding impairs memory formation, stress shortly before or after the presentation of new information generally enhances subsequent memory performance. In sharp contrast, <cover>stress before memory retrieval impairs the recall </cover>of information learned previously which may directly affect performance at exams.\" , \"start_char\": 7485.000000 , \"title\": \"Learning and memory under stress: implications for the classroom | npj Science of Learning\" , \"url\": \"https://www.nature.com/articles/npjscilearn201611\" }","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_48_36044806#8_82268102","rank":1,"relevance":3}]},"time":382},"model_calls":32,"document_judgments":25,"relevance_3":10,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
